 \section{Discussion}
 
-The principal hardware result was the stability of downstream classification despite measurable changes in the quantum kernels. Hardware execution produced substantially greater relative kernel error than exact or ideal simulation, particularly for the eight- and ten-qubit circuits, yet balanced accuracy changed by at most 1.67 percentage points in absolute value. The paired tests found no significant hardware-versus-exact prediction differences after multiplicity adjustment. These findings support empirical robustness for the tested samples, feature map, shot count, backend, and calibration period. They do not establish formal equivalence between exact and hardware execution.
+The principal hardware result was the stability of downstream classification despite measurable changes in the quantum kernels. Hardware execution produced substantially greater relative kernel error than exact or ideal simulation, particularly for the wider circuits, yet balanced accuracy changed by less than two percentage points. The paired tests found no significant hardware-versus-exact prediction differences after multiplicity adjustment. These findings show empirical stability for the tested samples, feature map, shot count, backend, and calibration period. They do not establish formal equivalence between exact and hardware execution.
 
-Kernel fidelity and classification performance were not interchangeable measures in this experiment. CZ count was strongly associated with kernel distortion but was not associated with accuracy degradation. The hardware kernels could therefore differ numerically from their exact counterparts while retaining enough relative structure for the SVC to produce similar decision boundaries. This observation is specific to the tested SVC solutions; greater distortion could affect another dataset, feature map, regularization value, or set of support vectors differently.
+Kernel fidelity and classification performance were not interchangeable measures in this experiment. Circuit resource requirements, represented here by CZ count, were strongly associated with kernel distortion but not with accuracy degradation. Because CZ count co-varied with circuit width, depth, and size, this analysis cannot identify CZ gates as the cause of the distortion. The hardware kernels could nevertheless differ numerically from their exact counterparts while retaining enough relative structure for the SVC to produce similar decision boundaries. This observation is specific to the tested SVC solutions; greater distortion could affect another dataset, feature map, regularization value, or set of support vectors differently.
 
-Feature compression presented a separate tradeoff. Four-component PCA reduced qubit count, circuit depth, CZ count, and hardware-induced kernel distortion. It also removed predictive information in some configurations. The effect was largest for CLaMP, where PCA-4 reduced hardware balanced accuracy by 10.0 points relative to native-10. MBR showed a smaller 2.9-point reduction. The results therefore do not support treating PCA solely as a noise-reduction step: its resource benefit must be evaluated against representation loss for each dataset.
+Feature compression presented a separate tradeoff. Four-component PCA reduced qubit count, circuit depth, CZ count, and hardware-induced kernel distortion. It also removed predictive information in some configurations, with a substantial loss for CLaMP and a smaller loss for MBR. The results therefore do not support treating PCA solely as a noise-reduction step: its resource benefit must be evaluated against representation loss for each dataset.
 
 The classical comparisons did not demonstrate a consistent accuracy advantage for the quantum kernel. RBF SVC was competitive with or better than the hardware-quantum model on CLaMP, CIC, and EMBER2018, while the quantum models were descriptively higher on MBR. These comparisons used fixed $C=1$ models without hyperparameter tuning and were intended as matched checks rather than optimized classical benchmarks. The study also did not collect comparable end-to-end wall-clock or system-level energy measurements. Accordingly, the results demonstrate neither predictive, computational, nor energy-based quantum advantage.
 
-The study has several limitations. Each split contained only 80 training and 80 test observations, and the balanced class distribution does not reflect operational malware prevalence. Only three repeated holdouts, one ZZ feature map, two repetitions, linear entanglement, 512 shots, one transpilation, and one hardware backend were evaluated. Error mitigation was disabled, and circuit width was coupled to dataset and representation, making the circuit-cost correlations descriptive rather than causal. The datasets also differ in observation type: CLaMP, MBR, and EMBER2018 describe PE files, whereas CIC represents sandbox activity records; MBR additionally depends on the provenance and version of its Kaggle source file. Finally, a post hoc CIC audit found two binary hashes recurring across different held-out splits, although no hash occurred in both training and test data within an individual split. Future work should use hash-, family-, session-, or time-grouped evaluation and repeat the experiment across devices, calibrations, feature maps, shot counts, and mitigation settings.
+\subsubsection{Limitations}
+
+The study has several limitations. Each split contained only 80 training and 80 test observations, and the balanced class distribution does not reflect operational malware prevalence. Only three repeated holdouts, one feature-map architecture, one shot count, one transpilation, and one hardware backend and calibration period were evaluated. Only CLaMP and MBR provided native-versus-PCA comparisons. Error mitigation was disabled, and circuit width was coupled to dataset and representation, making the circuit-resource correlations descriptive rather than causal. The classical baselines used fixed hyperparameters rather than optimization, and the study did not measure comparable wall-clock time or system-level energy use. The datasets also differ in observation type, and MBR depends on the provenance and version of its Kaggle source file. Finally, a post hoc CIC audit found repeated binary hashes across held-out splits, although none occurred in both training and test data within an individual split.
 
 \section{Conclusion}
 
-This study compared classical SVCs with exact, ideal-Aer, noisy-Aer, and IBM-hardware quantum kernels across four malware datasets. IBM hardware increasingly distorted the measured kernels as circuit width and CZ count increased, but downstream balanced accuracy remained close to exact-kernel performance in all six configurations. PCA reduced circuit cost and hardware kernel distortion while causing dataset-dependent losses in predictive performance. Classical RBF models remained competitive, and the experiment did not demonstrate quantum advantage in accuracy, speed, or energy use. The results instead provide a reproducible small-sample demonstration that quantum-kernel malware classification can remain stable on current NISQ hardware despite substantial kernel distortion.
+This study compared classical SVCs with exact, ideal-Aer, noisy-Aer, and IBM-hardware quantum kernels across four malware datasets. IBM hardware increasingly distorted the measured kernels as circuit resource requirements increased, but downstream balanced accuracy remained close to exact-kernel performance in all six configurations. PCA reduced circuit cost and hardware kernel distortion while causing dataset-dependent losses in predictive performance. Classical RBF models remained competitive, and the experiment did not demonstrate quantum advantage in accuracy, speed, or energy use. The results instead provide reproducible small-sample evidence that quantum-kernel malware classification can remain stable on current NISQ hardware despite substantial kernel distortion under the tested conditions.
+
+\subsubsection{Future Work}
 
+Future evaluations should use larger samples and further vary the tested configurations. Both the classical and quantum models may be improved through model tuning. Another useful comparison would be between this quantum support vector machine (QSVM) approach and a trainable quantum neural network (QNN) to test whether a learned representation improves predictive performance and exhibits the same sensitivity to hardware error.
 
+Another idea that emerged from this study is to use quantum--high-performance computing (quantum-HPC) methods to evaluate quantum-kernel scalability. For example, kernel evaluation could be parallelized across groups of quantum resources based on the size of the feature space. Such methods remained outside the scope of this study.
 
